      "side": 1,
      "message": "This needs to be just cidr_equals. \n\nSure, for some cases, there is a technical duplicate if you have two subnets, one encompassing the other. But EXPORT CLIENT list they may have different permissions. Like you can grant the smaller subnet different permissions or even a single address. So like you can have\n\n192.168.5.1 not allowed\n192.168.0.0/16 allowed\n\nI\u0027m not even sure how to specify the list that is allowed, but it\u0027s going to take more than 2 entries to do it. Now imagine the list of not allowed is 20 or 30 exceptions scattered about the address space...\n\nAnd when I delete, I want to be forced to delete the correct entry not just a super set or subset of it.\n\nSo I\u0027m OK with and inefficient list of:\n\n192.168.0.1, 192.168.0.0/16\n\nMaybe I\u0027m initially only looking at 192.168.0.1 and want to temporarily broaden to include the whole subnet, and then return to just tracing the single IP.\n\nActually, let\u0027s only use is_base_client_exact_match",
      "revId": "1ccca6c63fbe33e3fc420d64806dfcec0bdc414a",
